{"id": "example_000002", "text": "It is the third largest city of Bastar division. Kondagaon separated from Bastar district on 24 January 2012 and formed as 27th district of the Chhattisgarh state. It is mostly renowned for its bell metal craft and other art forms native to the tribal of Bastar. Also known as the Shilp sheher (lit. craft city) of Chhattisgarh owing to the variety of indigenous crafts produced in the area. MLA-Sh Mohan Lal Markam (Congress) Collector- Pushpendra Kumar Meena SP-Sh Sujit Kumar (IPS) CEO-Nupur Rashi Panna (IAS) Background Kondagaon is located at . It has an average elevation of 593 metres (1945 feet). India census, Kondagaon had a population of 40,921. Males constitute 50% of the population and females 50%. Kondagaon has an average literacy rate of 64%, higher than the national average of 59.5%: male literacy is 73%, and female literacy is 55%. In Kondagaon, 14% of the population is under 6 years of age. Kondagaon lies on the NH 30 highway and can be reached from either Raipur or Jagdalpur. Frequent bus services are available to Kondagaon from Raipur and Jagdalpur. The nearest railway station is Jagdalpur. A Helicopter strip was temporarily constructed in the playground adjacent to the government college campus, which is occasionally used in Kondagaon. The town is known for the foodies, being migrants and settlers from various parts of India including the south and the north. Lala hotel is the oldest hotel in the city operating through post independence era when kondagaon was as much of a small city. Kondagaon is situated on banks of river Narangi. Industries Art and Craft The Bell Metal Craft practiced by most of the craftsmen in the town and the adjacent villages is a form of almost extinct wax sculpting art. Some of the celebrated craftsmen of the bell-metal art forms are late Dr. Jaidev Baghel (a national awardee), Sushil Sakhuja, Sukchand, Suresh Baghmare etc. The government endow various schemes and policies to promote these art forms and artisans including sponsoring a select few second or third generation artisans for training in the national institute of design, enabling them to keep abreast with the trends and also widening the scope of their art forms. The local administration actively supports the continuation of these traditional skills through various government initiatives aimed at preserving cultural heritage. The practice of bell metal casting involves intricate processes beginning with the creation of a wax model, which is then meticulously encased in a clay mold before the metal is introduced in a lost-wax casting technique. This demanding artistry requires a deep understanding of metallurgy and artistic precision, skills that have been passed down through generations within the local community. Beyond bell metal, Kondagaon is a hub for numerous other indigenous crafts, reflecting the rich tapestry of Bastar's tribal culture. These include intricate tribal paintings, traditional weaving, and the making of wooden artifacts, each carrying specific cultural and symbolic significance. The local economy is heavily intertwined with the artisanal sector; many families derive their primary livelihood from producing and selling these handcrafted goods. Tourist influx, while still developing, offers a growing market for these unique products, providing a crucial economic lifeline to the artisans. Efforts are underway by regional tourism bodies to better integrate Kondagaon into broader cultural tourism circuits, thereby increasing visibility for its unique offerings. The culinary scene, mentioned previously due to its diverse migrant population, also forms a distinctive aspect of the town's social fabric, showcasing a confluence of regional Indian cuisines. Infrastructure development around the town continues to focus on improving connectivity and supporting local commerce. The ongoing upgrade of NH 30 aims to facilitate smoother transport for both residents and visitors interested in exploring the area's cultural wealth. Educational institutions, including the government college, serve not only as centers for modern learning but also as venues for cultural exhibitions and workshops, bridging the gap between traditional practices and contemporary education. The local governance structure attempts to balance the demands of modernization with the imperative of safeguarding the unique socio-cultural identity of Kondagaon and the surrounding tribal hamlets. The preservation of the bell metal craft is thus viewed not merely as an economic endeavor but as a critical act of cultural custodianship for the entire region."}
